"""Independent lessons following the mathematical sequence of book 14.1–14.4."""
import sympy as s
lessons=[];models=[]
def T(v):return s.latex(s.sympify(v))
def D(*rows):return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def E(code,title,question,solution,kind,**data):
 uid='MEQU-E-'+code;models.append(dict(id=uid,kind=kind,**data));return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def L(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
 lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='fm_equilibrium_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method='<ol>'+''.join('<li>'+m+'</li>' for m in method)+'</ol>',mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_equilibrium_'
L('moments','Moments, support reactions and composite rods','Balance turning effects as well as forces, with every weight at its own centre.',
 'Use moments and mass-weighted coordinates to determine support forces and centres of mass.',
 '<p>Review resolving forces, simultaneous equations and <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_projectiles_components">signed components</a>. Distinguish mass from weight. A rigid body has fixed shape; static equilibrium also requires no net turning effect.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A moment uses a perpendicular distance</h3><p>The moment of a force about a point measures its turning effect. Its magnitude is force times the perpendicular distance to its line of action. Choose one sign convention; below, anticlockwise is positive. With position relative to the chosen point and coplanar force components,</p>'''+D(r'M=xF_y-yF_x')+r'''<p>This formula follows by adding the moments of the horizontal and vertical components. A force through the chosen point has zero moment. The length of a sloping rod is not automatically the lever arm. Moments have units N m; they are signed, unlike their magnitudes.</p><h3>Three equations for a planar rigid body</h3>'''+D(r'\sum F_x=0,\qquad\sum F_y=0,\qquad\sum M_O=0')+r'''<p>Choose a moment point that removes unknown forces when possible. Force balance alone can leave a couple, and zero moment alone need not give zero resultant force. A hinge can exert horizontal and vertical components. A smooth contact supplies only a normal reaction; it cannot pull a body back onto the support.</p><h3>Replace distributed weight at the centre of mass</h3><p>A uniform straight rod has its centre at its midpoint. For separated components with masses and known centres, matching first moments gives</p>'''+D(r'\bar x=\frac{\sum m_i x_i}{\sum m_i},\qquad \bar y=\frac{\sum m_i y_i}{\sum m_i}')+r'''<p>Use masses, not merely lengths, unless all rods have the same mass per unit length. A light connector contributes no weight. Discs and spheres in a frame act at their own centres. A centre of mass can lie in an empty region of a frame.</p><h3>Test the support conditions</h3><p>After solving the equilibrium equations, check every one-sided reaction is non-negative. At a loss-of-contact threshold it can be zero. A negative result means the assumed contact is not physically possible; it is not a downward force from an ordinary support. Hinges are different because they can sustain either sign of component.</p><h3>Suspension of a composite frame</h3><p>In freely hanging equilibrium the centre of mass is vertically below the suspension point. Find its position in body-fixed axes first, then compare the suspension-to-centre line with the named side. Do not interchange an angle to the horizontal with its complementary angle.</p>''',
 ['List the bodies and all external forces with their points of action.','Take signed moments about a useful point and resolve forces in two directions.','For composite bodies, use the actual component masses and centres.','Solve for all requested force components or coordinates.','Check force signs, unilateral contacts, units and any parameter restrictions.'],
 ['Use perpendicular distance, not the distance along the rod, for a moment.','Zero resultant force does not by itself remove a turning couple.','A hinge reaction need not be perpendicular to the rod.','Equal lengths need not mean equal masses when linear densities differ.','A negative normal reaction invalidates a one-sided-contact assumption.'],[
 E('M1','1 · The sign changes with the moment point',r'<p>A force \((4,5)\) N acts at \((3,2)\) m. Find its signed moment about the origin and about \(P=(1,-1)\), using anticlockwise positive.</p>',D(r'M_O=3(5)-2(4)=7',r'\boldsymbol r_P=(2,3)',r'M_P=2(5)-3(4)=-2')+'<p>The first moment is 7 N m anticlockwise; the second has magnitude 2 N m clockwise. The force is unchanged, but its lever arm relative to the selected point changes.</p>','moment',point=[3,2],force=[4,5],pivot=[1,-1],values=[7,-2]),
 E('M2','2 · Both reactions of a loaded beam',r'<p>A uniform horizontal beam of length 5 m and weight 40 N rests on smooth supports at its ends. A 30 N load is attached 4 m from the left end. Find both upward reactions.</p>',D(r'5R_B=40\left(\frac52\right)+30(4)',r'R_B=44,\qquad R_A+R_B=70',r'R_A=26')+'<p>Both reactions are positive, so the assumed contacts are possible. Their sum balances the full 70 N load, not only the beam weight.</p>','beam',length=5,weight=40,load=30,position=4,reactions=['26','44']),
 E('M3','3 · A bracket with unequal linear densities',r'<p>Two uniform rods join at right angles: a horizontal rod from \((0,0)\) to \((4,0)\) has mass 2 kg, and a vertical rod to \((4,3)\) has mass 3 kg. Find the combined centre. If suspended from the origin, find the tangent of the acute angle between the originally horizontal rod and the downward vertical.</p>',D(r'G_1=(2,0),\qquad G_2=\left(4,\frac32\right)',r'\bar x=\frac{2(2)+3(4)}5=\frac{16}5',r'\bar y=\frac{3(3/2)}5=\frac9{10}',r'\tan\phi=\frac{\bar y}{\bar x}=\frac9{32}')+'<p>The rods have different mass per unit length, so weighting by length would be incorrect. In the hanging configuration the line from the origin to the combined centre points vertically down.</p>','wire',L=4,H=3,m1=2,m2=3,centre=['16/5','9/10'],tangent='9/32'),
 E('M4','4 · A cable and a two-component hinge force',r'<p>A horizontal rod of length 4 m and weight 60 N is hinged at \(A=(0,0)\). A cable joins \(B=(4,0)\) to \(D=(0,3)\). A 20 N load is fixed 3 m from A. Find the cable tension and both hinge components.</p>',D(r'4\left(\frac35T\right)=60(2)+20(3)',r'T=75',r'H=\frac45T=60',r'V=80-\frac35T=35')+'<p>The hinge acts rightwards and upwards. Taking moments about A removed its force from that equation; it did not imply that the hinge force was zero.</p>','hinge',L=4,H=3,weight=60,load=20,position=3,tension='75',horizontal='60',vertical='35')])
L('laminas','Plane laminas, suspension and toppling','Use signed areas for the centre, then test its line of weight against the support.',
 'Find centres of composite laminas and complete equilibrium ranges for suspension, tilting and cut shapes.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'moments">moments and weighted centres</a>. Review <a href="/courses/maths4u-9709-p1/topics/orig_quadratics_inequalities">quadratic inequalities</a>. Uniform lamina means constant mass per unit area, not necessarily equal masses for unequal pieces.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Areas can stand in for masses only under a common density</h3><p>A rectangle has its centre at its geometric centre; a uniform triangle has the average of its three vertex coordinates. For a composite uniform lamina, replace mass by area in the first-moment equations. A removed piece contributes negative area and negative first moment.</p>'''+D(r'\bar x=\frac{A_0x_0-A_hx_h}{A_0-A_h}',r'\bar y=\frac{A_0y_0-A_hy_h}{A_0-A_h}')+r'''<p>Choose disjoint pieces, or explicitly subtract overlaps. Keep the remaining area positive. A similar planar piece scaled by \(q\) has area scaled by \(q^2\), not \(q\). For a wire frame use line masses instead of filled-in areas.</p><h3>A named angle requires the correct reference line</h3><p>When suspended from \(S\), the vector from S to the centre of mass becomes vertically downward. The angle between a body side and the vertical is therefore the angle between that side and the S-to-centre line in the original diagram. Use the correct opposite and adjacent distances, including their signs when deciding the orientation.</p><h3>Toppling is a support-boundary condition</h3><p>On a horizontal supporting segment, the vertical projection of the centre must lie within the segment. At an endpoint the object is in limiting equilibrium. If a corner has been removed, the surviving base may be shorter than the original width.</p><h3>Tilting changes the projection, not the body-fixed centre</h3><p>Let the base run from downhill coordinate \(0\) to uphill coordinate \(b\), and let the centre have body-fixed coordinates \((\bar x,\bar y)\), with \(\bar y&gt;0\). If the base rises to the right at angle \(\alpha\), the weight line intersects it at</p>'''+D(r's=\bar x-\bar y\tan\alpha',r'0\le s\le b')+r'''<p>At downhill toppling, \(\tan\alpha_T=\bar x/\bar y\). A different supporting edge or tilt direction changes the geometry. Do not use a radius or half-width without identifying the actual pivot.</p><h3>Sliding can happen before toppling</h3><p>When weight and the plane contact are the only external forces, the required friction-to-normal ratio is \(\tan\alpha\). Thus no sliding requires \(\tan\alpha\le\mu\). Compare this threshold with the toppling threshold. Saying “friction is sufficient” is an assumption; otherwise check it.</p>'''.replace('&gt;',r'\gt '),
 ['Select body-fixed axes and disjoint positive and removed pieces.','Calculate both first moments using area or the actual masses.','For hanging, compare the suspension-to-centre line with the named side.','For standing or tilting, locate the weight-line projection on the actual base.','Intersect geometric, positivity, friction and endpoint conditions.'],
 ['A hole subtracts both mass and first moment.','The centre of a triangle is not generally halfway up its height.','The suspension angle to one side may be complementary to another named angle.','Toppling depends on the surviving support, not the outline before cutting.','Check sliding separately before claiming that a toppling threshold is reached.'],[
 E('L1','1 · An off-centre rectangular hole',r'<p>A uniform \(6\) cm by \(4\) cm rectangle occupies \(0\le x\le6,\ 0\le y\le4\). A \(2\) cm by \(2\) cm rectangle centred at \((4,2)\) is removed. Find the remaining centre of mass.</p>',D(r'A=24-4=20',r'20\bar x=24(3)-4(4)=56',r'20\bar y=24(2)-4(2)=40',r'G=\left(\frac{14}5,2\right)')+'<p>The hole is to the right of the original centre, so the remaining centre moves left. The horizontal symmetry through y=2 is preserved.</p>','hole',width=6,height=4,holeArea=4,holeCentre=[4,2],centre=['14/5','2']),
 E('L2','2 · Read the suspension angle from the geometry',r'<p>A uniform lamina is formed from the triangle with vertices \((0,0),(6,0),(6,3)\) and the rectangle \(6\le x\le8,\ 0\le y\le3\). It is held rigidly as one body and suspended from \(S=(0,3)\). Find its centre and the tangent of the angle between the originally downward side from S to the origin and the downward vertical.</p>',D(r'A_1=9,\quad G_1=(4,1)',r'A_2=6,\quad G_2=\left(7,\frac32\right)',r'\bar x=\frac{9(4)+6(7)}{15}=\frac{26}5',r'\bar y=\frac{9(1)+6(3/2)}{15}=\frac65',r'G=\left(\frac{26}5,\frac65\right)',r'\tan\phi=\frac{26/5}{3-6/5}=\frac{26}9')+'<p>The two pieces share their vertical joining edge. The vertical separation is measured from S, not from the original horizontal axis.</p>','suspension',masses=[9,6],centres=[['4','1'],['7','3/2']],pivot=['0','3'],centre=['26/5','6/5'],tangent='26/9'),
 E('L3','3 · Compare sliding with toppling',r'<p>A uniform rectangular frame of mass m has a base from \((0,0)\) to \((4,0)\) and centre \((2,3)\). A particle of mass \(\frac m2\) is fixed at \((4,3)\). The base is tilted upwards to the right, with friction coefficient \(\frac12\). Which limit is reached first?</p>',D(r'\bar x=\frac{2+4/2}{1+1/2}=\frac83,\qquad\bar y=3',r'\tan\alpha_T=\frac{8}{9}',r'\tan\alpha_S=\frac12\lt\frac89')+'<p>Sliding occurs first. The toppling calculation assumed contact could hold without sliding; the stated friction is not enough to reach that inclination.</p>','tilt',masses=['1','1/2'],centres=[['2','3'],['4','3']],width=4,mu='1/2',centre=['8/3','3'],topple='8/9'),
 E('L4','4 · A complete range after a corner is cut away',r'<p>From a uniform square \(0\le x,y\le6\), remove the triangle with vertices \((0,0),(h,0),(0,6)\), where \(0\lt h\lt6\). The remainder stands on the surviving bottom segment \(h\le x\le6\). Find the complete allowed h-range, including limiting equilibrium.</p>',D(r'A=36-3h',r'\bar x=\frac{108-h^2}{36-3h}',r'\bar x\ge h\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad h^2-18h+54\ge0',r'h=9\pm3\sqrt3',r'0\lt h\le9-3\sqrt3')+r'<p>The upper condition \(\bar x\le6\) holds throughout the original geometric domain: \(h^2-18h+108\gt0\). The smaller boundary is where the weight line reaches the newly cut base endpoint.</p>','cut_range',width=6,height=6,centroidX='(108-h**2)/(36-3*h)',upper='9-3*sqrt(3)')])
L('solids','Composite solids, cavities and shells','Use volumes or surface areas consistently, and measure every centre from one reference.',
 'Calculate centres of three-dimensional bodies and apply the correct suspension or toppling geometry.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'laminas">signed first moments and toppling</a>. Review volumes, surface areas and similarity. Standard cone and hemisphere centre formulas may be used when stated; deriving them by integration is not required here.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Use the right physical measure</h3><p>For uniform solids of the same density, masses are proportional to volumes. For thin uniform shells of the same surface density, masses are proportional to material surface areas. An axial drawing shows geometry; its areas are not the masses of the three-dimensional components. Different densities must remain as factors.</p><h3>Standard centres and a common axial coordinate</h3><p>For a uniform cylinder the centre is halfway along its height. A solid cone of height h has its centre h/4 from the base, or 3h/4 from the vertex. A solid hemisphere of radius R has its centre 3R/8 from its plane face; a thin hemispherical shell has its centre R/2 from that face. These refer to different physical objects.</p>'''+D(r'\bar z=\frac{\sum m_i z_i}{\sum m_i}')+r'''<p>Convert every quoted distance to the same origin and direction before combining it. A cone added above a cylinder has its centre above their joining plane, not above the cylinder's lower base by h/4 alone.</p><h3>Cavities and frusta</h3><p>A cavity subtracts mass and its own first moment. A blind bore has the centre of the removed cylinder at half its depth from the opening. A frustum can be treated as a larger cone minus a smaller similar cone. Use the cube of the linear scale factor for the solid-cone mass ratio.</p><h3>Count the shell surfaces actually present</h3><p>List lateral surfaces and each included end face separately. A circular joining face contributes mass only if the question retains it. A hemisphere's curved shell area is not its solid volume. The shell examples below state whether a hidden joining face exists.</p><h3>Equilibrium requires the right geometric projection</h3><p>For an axisymmetric body with centre height d above a circular base of radius R on a rough incline, downhill toppling occurs at \(d\tan\alpha=R\). Friction must also prevent sliding. When hanging from a rim point, use the centre's signed distance from the plane containing that rim. A curved contact can require a vertical through the contact point instead; the circular-base rule is not universal.</p>''',
 ['List real material components and removed regions with their density factors.','Choose one reference plane and write every component centre in that coordinate.','Use volume for solids and material area for shells.','Apply signed first moments and verify the remaining mass is positive.','Use the actual suspension point or support geometry and retain every admissible parameter root.'],
 ['An axial cross-sectional area does not weight the masses of solid components.','A cone centre quoted from the vertex differs from the one quoted from its base.','A blind bore is not a hole through the full height.','A closed cylinder has end faces; an open joining rim does not create a new face.','A real algebraic root must still satisfy cavity-fit and geometric conditions.'],[
 E('S1','1 · A cone above a cylinder',r'<p>A solid cylinder of radius 2 cm and height 6 cm carries a coaxial solid cone of the same radius and height 3 cm. Both have the same density. Use the cone centre at one quarter of its height above its base. Find the combined centre height and the toppling inclination on a sufficiently rough plane.</p>',D(r'V_{\rm cyl}=24\pi,\quad z_{\rm cyl}=3',r'V_{\rm cone}=4\pi,\quad z_{\rm cone}=6+\frac34=\frac{27}4',r'\bar z=\frac{24(3)+4(27/4)}{28}=\frac{99}{28}',r'\tan\alpha_T=\frac2{\bar z}=\frac{56}{99}')+'<p>The common density and π cancel. The cone centre must include the height of the cylinder underneath it.</p>','cylinder_cone',radius=2,cylinder=6,cone=3,height='99/28',topple='56/99'),
 E('S2','2 · A blind bore measured from the top',r'<p>A cylinder of radius 3 cm and height 8 cm has a coaxial blind cylindrical bore of radius 1 cm and depth 4 cm, opening at the top. Find the remaining centre height above the base and its distance below the top.</p>',D(r'V_0=72\pi,\quad z_0=4',r'V_h=4\pi,\quad z_h=8-\frac42=6',r'\bar z=\frac{72(4)-4(6)}{68}=\frac{66}{17}',r'd=8-\bar z=\frac{70}{17}')+'<p>The removed material is above the original centre, so the remaining centre moves down. Its distance below the top consequently increases.</p>','bore',radius=3,H=8,holeRadius=1,depth=4,height='66/17',below='70/17'),
 E('S3','3 · A frustum from two similar cones',r'<p>A uniform solid frustum has lower radius \(3r\), upper radius r and height h. Find its centre height from the lower face. If \(h=\frac{13r}3\) and it hangs from a lower rim point, find the tangent of the angle between a base diameter through that point and the downward vertical.</p>',D(r'H_{\rm large}=\frac32h,\qquad H_{\rm small}=\frac12h',r'm_{\rm large}:m_{\rm small}=27:1',r'z_{\rm large}=\frac38h,\qquad z_{\rm small}=h+\frac18h',r'\bar z=\frac{27(3h/8)-9h/8}{26}=\frac9{26}h',r'\tan\phi=\frac{\bar z}{3r}=\frac12')+'<p>The subtracted cone centre is measured from the lower face too. In the suspension calculation the horizontal offset is a radius, not a diameter.</p>','frustum',lower=3,upper=1,heightFactor='9/26',aspect='13/3',tangent='1/2'),
 E('S4','4 · An open joining rim is not an extra disc',r'<p>A thin cylindrical shell of radius a and height h has a lower circular face but no top face. A thin hemispherical cap of radius a joins its top rim; there is no internal circular face. All surfaces have the same mass per unit area. Use the cap centre a/2 above its plane face. Find the combined centre and the h-range for equilibrium on a rough incline with \(\tan\alpha=\frac12\), assuming sliding is prevented.</p>',D(r'A_{\rm side}=2\pi ah,\quad A_{\rm bottom}=\pi a^2',r'A_{\rm cap}=2\pi a^2',r'\bar z=\frac{ah^2+2a^2h+a^3}{2ah+3a^2}',r'\bar z=\frac{(h+a)^2}{2h+3a}',r'\frac12\bar z\le a\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad h^2-2ah-5a^2\le0',r'0\lt h\le(1+\sqrt6)a')+'<p>The top rim has no circular disc, so adding one would change both total mass and first moment. The upper endpoint is included because limiting toppling is allowed.</p>','shell',height='(h+a)**2/(2*h+3*a)',upper='1+sqrt(6)')])
L('contacts','Contact forces, friction and linked constraints','Find the required forces before imposing friction limits or an elastic-string law.',
 'Construct complete force-and-moment models and check all contact, friction and extension conditions.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'moments">moments and reactions</a> and <a href="'+base+'laminas">centres and toppling</a>. Three written applications also use Hooke’s law, a later-topic prerequisite stated below. No dynamics of rotation or moment of inertia is needed in this chapter.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Draw a separate force at every contact</h3><p>A smooth plane supplies a force perpendicular to its surface. A smooth peg against a straight rod pushes perpendicular to the rod. A smooth circular surface pushes along its contact normal, through the circle centre. A hinge can have two components; a taut string pulls along its own length. Use the actual geometry to determine these directions.</p><h3>Friction is an inequality except at a specified limit</h3>'''+D(r'|F|\le\mu N,\qquad N\ge0')+r'''<p>The sign of F comes from the tendency to slip or from the equilibrium equations. In limiting equilibrium, use equality with the specified impending-motion direction. At two rough contacts, each friction force has its own normal and coefficient; do not mix the two ratios. A coefficient need not be less than one unless the question says so.</p><h3>Moments, then force balance, then physical checks</h3><p>Choose a moment point that removes as many unknown reactions as possible. Resolve forces in convenient perpendicular directions and solve. Only after that compare the required friction with its allowed maximum, check normals are non-negative, and ensure a contact point lies on the actual rod or segment. Do not assume that friction always acts towards a wall.</p><h3>When a string is elastic</h3><p>For a light elastic string of natural length \(\ell_0\), extended length \(\ell&gt;\ell_0\) and modulus \(\lambda\),</p>'''+D(r'T=\lambda\frac{\ell-\ell_0}{\ell_0}')+r'''<p>Determine the equilibrium tension from moments and force balance, then use the actual extension in Hooke's law. Modulus has units of force. A string cannot provide compressive tension. With a rough ring, analyse the ring separately as well as the attached rod; their external forces are not identical.</p><h3>Curved bodies still need moment balance</h3><p>At a circular contact the normal passes through the centre, so its moment about the centre is zero. Tangential friction and a string attached elsewhere can still have nonzero moments. A horizontal pulling force does not automatically act through the centre. Check all signed lever arms before resolving parallel and perpendicular to an incline.</p><h3>Compare all possible failure boundaries</h3><p>A mathematically solved force system may still require a negative normal, a slack string or excessive friction. A contact can be lost before another friction limit is reached. Report the whole allowed parameter interval by intersecting every relevant condition.</p>'''.replace('&gt;',r'\gt '),
 ['Identify each contact normal, friction direction, hinge component and string force.','Write moment and force equations for the chosen body; separate connected bodies when needed.','Apply limiting friction only where the problem specifies the limit.','Use Hooke’s law only after finding the tension and actual extension.','Check all normals, friction bounds, geometry and parameter domains.'],
 ['A smooth peg reaction is perpendicular to the contacting rod, not necessarily vertical.','Friction is not automatically at its maximum in ordinary equilibrium.','Each contact has its own normal reaction in the friction ratio.','Use extension divided by natural length, not total string length, in Hooke’s law.','A force through one moment point can still affect the force-balance equations.'],[
 E('C1','1 · A smooth wall and a rough floor',r'<p>A uniform ladder of weight 100 N rests against a smooth vertical wall. Its inclination has \(\tan\theta=\frac34\). Find the wall reaction, floor normal, required friction and least floor coefficient.</p>',D(r'R_B(L\sin\theta)=100\left(\frac L2\cos\theta\right)',r'R_B=\frac{100}{2\tan\theta}=\frac{200}3',r'N=100,\quad F=\frac{200}3,\quad\mu_{\min}=\frac23')+'<p>The wall pushes away from itself, so floor friction acts towards it in this particular configuration. Equality in the friction law describes the least possible coefficient, not every equilibrium coefficient.</p>','ladder',weight=100,tangent='3/4',normal='100',wall='200/3',friction='200/3',mu='2/3'),
 E('C2','2 · A peg gives a different friction direction',r'<p>A uniform rod of length \(5a\) and weight W rises to the right with \(\tan\theta=\frac43\). Its lower end rests on rough horizontal ground. A smooth peg at distance \(3a\) along the rod pushes perpendicular to it, upwards-left. Find the peg reaction, floor normal, friction and least coefficient.</p>',D(r'3aR=W\left(\frac{5a}{2}\right)\frac35,\qquad R=\frac W2',r'F=\frac45R=\frac25W\quad\text{rightwards}',r'N=W-\frac35R=\frac7{10}W',r'\mu_{\min}=\frac FN=\frac47')+'<p>The peg has a leftward component, so the floor friction here acts rightwards. All normal reactions are positive.</p>','peg',length=5,contact=3,weight='W',tangent='4/3',reaction='W/2',normal='7*W/10',friction='2*W/5',mu='4/7'),
 E('C3','3 · Equilibrium tension before the elastic law',r'<p>A horizontal rod of length \(6a\) and weight W is hinged at its left end. A cable runs from the right end to a wall point \(8a\) above the hinge. A load \(\frac W2\) acts \(4a\) from the hinge. The cable is an elastic string of natural length \(8a\). Find T, both hinge components and its modulus.</p>',D(r'6a\left(\frac45T\right)=3aW+4a\left(\frac W2\right)',r'T=\frac{25}{24}W',r'H=\frac35T=\frac58W',r'V=\frac32W-\frac45T=\frac23W',r'\ell=10a,\qquad\lambda=T\frac{8a}{2a}=\frac{25}6W')+'<p>The current length is obtained from the 6–8–10 triangle. Tension comes from equilibrium; the modulus then follows from the positive extension.</p>','elastic',L=6,H=8,natural=8,loadRatio='1/2',position=4,tension='25/24',horizontal='5/8',vertical='2/3',modulus='25/6'),
 E('C4','4 · A circular contact still has a friction moment',r'<p>A thin ring of radius a and weight W rests on a rough plane rising rightwards with \(\tan\alpha=\frac34\). A horizontal string pulls rightwards at the point whose radius is parallel to the plane on the upslope side. Find the tension and the coefficient at limiting equilibrium.</p>',D(r'\sin\alpha=\frac35,\qquad\cos\alpha=\frac45',r'aF=aT\sin\alpha',r'T\cos\alpha+F=W\sin\alpha',r'T=\frac37W,\quad F=\frac9{35}W',r'N=W\cos\alpha+T\sin\alpha=\frac{37}{35}W',r'\mu=\frac9{37}')+'<p>The normal passes through the ring centre, but the friction and off-centre string do not. Friction acts upslope in this configuration; its moment is essential.</p>','ring',tangent='3/4',tension='3/7',normal='37/35',friction='9/35',mu='9/37')])
